Title: Thin the wording-pin tests: first bounded batch, and the remedy in pulse's toolkit
Created: 2026-10-21
Author: reviewer

--- log ---
2026-10-21T15:05Z reviewer created

--- body ---
## Context
At the author's request (2026-10-21, "dispatch the wording-pin test cutter"): the test suite has many
tests that only pin a sentence of a skill or rule ("this phrase is in that file"). A rough count
(a grep for `assert ... in <text>` shapes, not a measurement): 57 of 161 test files. They make every
wording change a two-file change, and they drove several review rounds on 2026-10-21 (PR 1557 took six
heads). The harness's own rule applies: when a guard misfires, prefer deleting the guard and its tests
to growing them (`rules/workflow.md`, severity floor).

Not a new skill: the testing doctor `/pulse` exists. This ticket delivers (1) the first bounded cut and
(2) the remedy and its syndrome as short entries in `skills/pulse/references/toolkit.md` and
`syndromes.md` (references: no resident cost; the skill card budget has about 4 characters spare).

## Exit criteria
- [ ] Classify the pin-style tests in one list (in the PR body): KEEP behaviour tests; structural
      invariants (frontmatter, catalog, personal data, agnostic checks); tests tied to a real past
      incident, named by ticket or PR (for example the frozen never-APPROVED allowlist in
      `tests/test_gaze_never_approves.py`); CUT tests whose only assertion is that a phrase
      appears in a skill, rule or doc, with no behaviour behind it.
- [ ] One bounded batch is cut: at most 15 files in the PR, deleted whole where the file is only pins,
      trimmed where a file mixes pins and behaviour. Each deletion has a one-line reason in the PR body.
- [ ] A check per cut that nothing else depended on it: the full suite passes; a test that imported a
      helper from a deleted test file is repaired; no remaining test or script references a deleted
      test by name (`git grep`).
- [ ] Measured: test count and `make check` wall time before and after, from the same host, in the PR body.
- [ ] `skills/pulse/references/syndromes.md` gets a syndrome ("a testbase that pins prose") and
      `references/toolkit.md` a remedy ("thin the wording pins": the keep and cut classes above, a bounded
      batch per PR, a reason per deletion, a reviewer samples the cuts); a few lines each.
- [ ] No frozen allowlist, incident-tied test or behaviour test is weakened; the PR body lists what was
      deliberately left.
- [ ] Nothing here requires a particular agent runtime; PR under the 15-file breaker (further batches are
      further PRs, not part of this ticket).
